1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,695
Oil helps your carrots.

2
00:00:01,695 --> 00:00:03,241
Heat can cost your
peppers.

3
00:00:03,841 --> 00:00:04,831
One pan.

4
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:06,047
Two opposite things

5
00:00:06,047 --> 00:00:07,627
happening to your
veggies.

6
00:00:08,227 --> 00:00:10,733
Bright veggies like
carrots are full of

7
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:13,003
plant pigments called
carotenoids.

8
00:00:13,003 --> 00:00:15,306
Your body turns them
into vitamin A.

9
00:00:16,006 --> 00:00:17,120
Here's the catch.

10
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:18,752
Those pigments get
absorbed

11
00:00:18,752 --> 00:00:20,209
better with a little
fat.

12
00:00:20,809 --> 00:00:22,248
Think of it like a ride.

13
00:00:22,248 --> 00:00:24,377
The pigments travel
along with the oil.

14
00:00:24,977 --> 00:00:27,469
So a splash of oil isn't
just for flavor.

15
00:00:28,069 --> 00:00:30,602
Same story for vitamins
E and K,

16
00:00:30,602 --> 00:00:32,584
in spinach and broccoli.

17
00:00:32,584 --> 00:00:33,722
Fat helps them in.

18
00:00:34,622 --> 00:00:35,952
Now the flip side.

19
00:00:35,952 --> 00:00:38,509
Peppers, broccoli and
tomatoes are

20
00:00:38,509 --> 00:00:40,117
packed with vitamin C.

21
00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:42,973
Vitamin C dissolves in
water,

22
00:00:42,973 --> 00:00:45,907
and cooking can lower
how much is left.

23
00:00:46,607 --> 00:00:49,591
Microwaving and steaming
can lower it too,

24
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:51,325
so it's not just the
pan.

25
00:00:51,925 --> 00:00:53,927
That's why raw fruit and
veg

26
00:00:53,927 --> 00:00:55,583
top the vitamin C lists.

27
00:00:56,483 --> 00:00:58,578
So sautéing isn't good
or bad.

28
00:00:58,578 --> 00:00:59,508
It's a trade.

29
00:01:00,108 --> 00:01:01,685
This isn't a diet rule.

30
00:01:01,685 --> 00:01:03,599
It's just what your pan
is doing.

31
00:01:04,199 --> 00:01:07,062
The Pan Theory: carrots
want a little oil.

32
00:01:07,062 --> 00:01:08,873
Peppers can keep more C
raw.

